This logic assumes that the last record in the table array is the one just created, which is fragile and could break with concurrent operations or if the InMemoryDriver's internal behavior changes. Consider finding the record by comparing the result.id instead of using array indexing. For example, you could use `table.find(r => r.id === result.id)` to be more robust.
```suggestion
    // Patch the stored record in-place by matching on id
    const table = (driver as any).db[object] as any[] | undefined;
    if (table && result?.id != null) {
      const stored = table.find((record: any) => record && record.id === result.id);
      if (stored) {
        stored._id = stored.id ?? result.id;
```

The patchDriverCreate function only patches the create() method, but update() and other methods that modify records may also need to maintain the `_id === id` invariant. If a record is updated through the driver directly (as seen in ObjectForm.test.tsx line 62: `driver.update('contact', originalAlice.id, originalAlice)`), the `_id` field might not be synchronized. Consider whether update() operations also need patching, or document why they don't.
```suggestion
  const originalUpdate = (driver as any).update
    ? (driver as any).update.bind(driver)
    : undefined;

  (driver as any).create = async (object: string, data: any, options?: any) => {
    const result = await originalCreate(object, data, options);
    // Patch the stored record in-place for newly created records
    const table = (driver as any).db[object] as any[] | undefined;
    if (table) {
      const stored = table[table.length - 1];
      if (stored && stored.id === result.id && stored.id) {
        stored._id = stored.id;
      }
    }
    // Also patch the returned copy
    if (result && result.id) {
      result._id = result.id;
    }
    return result;
  };

  // Also patch update so that `_id` stays in sync with `id` after mutations
  if (originalUpdate) {
    (driver as any).update = async (
      object: string,
      id: any,
      data: any,
      options?: any,
    ) => {
      const result = await originalUpdate(object, id, data, options);

      // Ensure the stored record has `_id === id`
      const table = (driver as any).db[object] as any[] | undefined;
      if (table) {
        const stored = table.find(
          (record: any) => record.id === id || record._id === id,
        );
        if (stored && stored.id) {
          stored._id = stored.id;
        }
      }

      // Ensure the returned record also has `_id === id`
      if (result && result.id) {
        result._id = result.id;
      }

      return result;
    };
  }
```
